<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uxama Argaela</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Uxama</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uxama Argaela was a town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arevaci">Arevaci</a> near modern El Burgo de Osma in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soria">Soria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. A Celtiberian stronghold, it became a Roman municipium and later the seat of the bishopric of Osma, which takes its name from it. Excavations on its hilltop site have revealed houses, cisterns and public buildings of Roman date.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uxama-argaela.jpg" alt="Uxama Argaela" width="320" height="177" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uxama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>As one of the cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arevaci">Arevaci</a>, Uxama took an active part in the Celtiberian Wars of 153–133 BCE and was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 99 BCE. It later supported the cause of the Roman rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Quintus Sertorius</a> and was destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> the Great in 72 BCE, though it was rebuilt shortly afterwards. According to Pliny and Ptolemy it was one of the communities of the Conventus Iuridicus Cluniensis in Hispania Tarraconensis.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> it became a municipium, and an important programme of monumental building followed: a small forum, a series of large urban villas, city walls and an industrial district on the banks of the river Ucero. In the 6th century, in the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> attended the Councils of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The city lies on El Castro hill, overlooking the present town of El Burgo de Osma, and covers two plateaus with an area of 28 hectares. Its walls, protected by rectangular towers, can be seen to the north-east and south. The main monuments are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Mina</a>, a section of a drain, cisterns, baths and a basilica with mosaics, and sections of the aqueduct were cut as tunnels into the solid rock at the edge of the city. In the Portugui vineyards part of an extensive Celtiberian cemetery has been excavated, with cremation graves of the 3rd and 2nd centuries BCE.</p><p>A season of excavations is held every summer together with the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiermes">Tiermes</a>, and a small museum, open only in the summer months, stands about 2 kilometres from the site. The finds, among them sculptures, Roman capitals, iron weapons, inscriptions, republican and imperial coins, pottery, glass and a set of horse bronzes, are shown in the National Archaeological Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, the Numantine Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soria">Soria</a> and the National Army Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uxama Argaela” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Over the following centuries the scattered hillforts grew into larger fortified towns, and by the third century BCE the Celtiberians lived in a landscape of oppida such as Numantia, Termes and <b>Uxama</b> among the Arevaci, Segeda among the Belli and Contrebia Belaisca in the Ebro valley.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Strabo called them the most powerful of the Celtiberians, and their towns included Numantia, Termes and <b>Uxama</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/arevaci">Arevaci</a></li></ul></section>
